  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "When a bucket is two-thirds full of water, the bucket and water weigh \\(a\\) kilograms. When the bucket is one-half full of water the total weight is \\(b\\) kilograms. In terms of \\(a\\) and \\(b,\\) what is the total weight in kilograms when the bucket is full of water?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{3}a + \\dfrac{1}{3}b\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}a - \\dfrac{1}{2}b\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}a + b\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}a + 2b\\)",
    "E": "\\(3a - 2b\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The bucket itself has the same weight in both measurements, so the difference \\(a-b\\) comes entirely from the extra water. The difference in fill levels is\n\\[\n\\frac23-\\frac12=\\frac16.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\frac16\\) of the bucket's capacity of water weighs \\(a-b\\) kilograms.\n\nStarting from the two-thirds-full measurement, filling the bucket requires another \\(\\frac13\\) of its capacity. This is twice \\(\\frac16\\), so the added water weighs \\(2(a-b)\\) kilograms. The full bucket therefore weighs\n\\[\na+2(a-b)=\\boxed{3a-2b}\n\\]\nkilograms, choice E.",
